he shared-referral arrangement.** Your existing in-house referral flow alone clears the annual costs 6x over and pays back the capital in under 2.5 years — which is fast for equipment with a 7–10 year useful life. The idle time during the week is irrelevant to this math; it's a capacity/utilization question, not a profitability one, since the $9k in fixed costs is already covered many times over by current volume.\n\nTreat the neighboring practice's shared-referral offer as **upside to pursue in parallel, not a precondition** — it fills otherwise-idle capacity at near-zero marginal capital cost, but making the purchase contingent on it just delays cash flow you're already positioned to capture, and risks losing exclusivity if they buy their own machine while you're negotiating.\n\nBefore signing, I'd verify three things that materially change the math if wrong:\n1. Is $180 a *net* margin (after any radiologist read fee or consumables) or gross charge? If there's a real per-scan cost, payback stretches but likely stays under 3 years.\n2. Is the 25/month referral volume stable/growing, or seasonal/lumpy?\n3. Who operates the machine and reads the scans — does this eat a dentist's or hygienist's billable time, which would offset some of that $180?\n\nIf those hold up, this is a clear buy.","ttft_ms":26860,"type":"result","duration_ms":34123,"uuid":"1f61b56d-5347-4ed4-ac57-5d4e18a5c1d3","ttft_stream_ms":1880,"time_to_request_ms":53,"first_content_frame_ms":1880,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
